<p>The United States is the anchor market for the entire Indian shrimp industry. It took 311,948 MT of Indian frozen shrimp in FY2024-25, more than double the next-largest market (China, at 136,164 MT), according to MPEDA's own official press release. Total Indian frozen shrimp exports that year reached 741,529 MT worth roughly USD 5.18 billion, and within the USA's total seafood import basket from India (USD 2,714.94 million across 346,868 MT), frozen shrimp made up 92.55% of the value. This guide is for US importers, distributors and foodservice buyers sourcing farmed vannamei from India: the forms and counts on offer, how glaze affects what you actually pay for, the FDA paperwork, and what goes wrong on a first order.</p>

<!-- slot:facts -->

<!-- insight:buyer-segmentation -->
<h2>Do US buyers pay more for Indian shrimp than other markets?</h2>
<p>Yes, on average. MPEDA does not publish shrimp-only value by country, but its FY2024-25 release gives enough to work it out. The US took USD 2,714.94 million of Indian seafood, and frozen shrimp was 92.55% of that value, about USD 2,513 million. Divided by the 311,948 MT shipped, that is roughly USD 8.05 per kg. India's frozen shrimp exports as a whole averaged about USD 6.98 per kg (USD 5,177.01 million across 741,529 MT). The US paid about 15% more per kilo than the average, taking 42% of the volume and about 49% of the value.</p>
<p>That premium reflects what the US buys: more peeled and value-added forms (PD, PDTO, cooked), retail packs and larger counts, against a larger share of HOSO and HLSO in some other markets. A unit value is an average across every form and count, not a price. Scenario: a Texas foodservice distributor buying 26/30 PDTO in 2kg retail-ready inners and a Houston ethnic-retail importer buying HOSO 40/50 in blocks are both "US shrimp buyers", but their prices per kilo will be far apart, and neither should benchmark against the other.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

<h2>Which shrimp form and count should a US buyer order?</h2>
<p>Vannamei (L. vannamei) ships in several forms — HOSO (head-on shell-on), HLSO (headless shell-on), PD (peeled deveined), PUD (peeled undeveined), PDTO (peeled deveined tail-on) and cooked — each with a different yield and price. Count size (pieces per pound) is the other major price driver: 16/20 commands a premium over 71/90 because larger shrimp cost more to farm to size. Specify both before you request a price, and be wary of any comparison between quotes that don't match on form and count. IQF suits retail and foodservice packs; block frozen suits further processing and costs less to pack.</p>

<!-- insight:cost-buildup -->
<h2>How does glaze change what you actually pay per kilo?</h2>
<p>This is the single most common source of confusion in shrimp pricing, and it's worth understanding in detail. Glazing is a thin ice coating applied after freezing to prevent freezer burn during storage and transit — typically 0%, 10% or 20% of gross weight. A supplier quoting on gross weight with 20% glaze is effectively offering roughly 20% less actual shrimp than the same number quoted on net weight. We quote net weight and state the glaze percentage separately, and we'd recommend insisting on the same clarity from any supplier you're comparing us against.</p>
<p>The cost per kilo of shrimp rises faster than the glaze percentage. With 20% glaze, each gross kilo holds 0.8 kg of shrimp, so the price per net kilo is the gross price divided by 0.8: 25% higher, not 20%. At 10% glaze it is about 11% higher. Form adds the next layer. Using the typical yields in the comparison table below, a kilo of PD at 55-60% of HOSO weight takes about 1.7-1.8 kg of HOSO shrimp, and PDTO at 50-55% takes about 1.8-2.0 kg. Add phosphate treatment to the check: shrimp soaked in STPP or another phosphate holds more water, which adds weight you pay for. Ask whether it is used, and at what level.</p>
<p>Scenario: two offers for PD 31/40 arrive at the same headline price. One is net weight with 0% glaze; the other is gross weight with 20% glaze. The second is 25% dearer per kilo of shrimp, before any difference in phosphate or count tolerance.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

<!-- insight:container-economics -->
<h2>How much shrimp fits in a 40ft reefer to the US?</h2>
<p>About 25-27 MT in a 40ft high-cube reefer against 12-14 MT in a 20ft, so a 40ft carries roughly twice as much. It is the cheaper box per tonne whenever its freight rate is under twice the 20ft reefer rate. The carton configuration and glaze set where in each range you land.</p>
<p>The cargo value is what makes the cold chain matter. At the US average of about USD 8.05 per kg, a 26 MT 40ft carries roughly USD 210,000 of shrimp. A power failure at a transshipment port, or a week's detention while a residue test comes back, puts that whole value at risk. A temperature data logger costs little next to that, and so does a pre-shipment residue test.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

<h2>How long does shipping take, and does shelf life hold?</h2>
<p>Transit time depends heavily on which US port you're landing at. East Coast ports — New York/New Jersey and Savannah — typically run 25-35 days from Indian ports. West Coast routing via Panama/Suez or transshipment can be faster, 22-30 days. Houston runs longest at 28-36 days. None of this is a quality issue — frozen product at -18°C holds its 18-24 month shelf life comfortably across any of these windows — but it does mean your reorder planning needs longer lead times than a Gulf or Southeast Asian shipment would require. Most export vannamei is farmed in Andhra Pradesh, and the MPEDA figures above cover the whole Indian industry.</p>

<!-- slot:docs -->

<p>US food imports run through FSMA (the Food Safety Modernization Act), and two pieces of it apply directly to a shrimp shipment. The first is FSVP (the Foreign Supplier Verification Program) — your obligation as the US importer, requiring you to verify that your foreign suppliers meet US standards. We provide the documentation and process transparency you'll need to satisfy it. The second is FDA's Seafood HACCP regulation (21 CFR Part 123), under which the processing plan for the product is something FDA can inspect or request records against, and which your FSVP verification will draw on.</p>
<!-- insight:documentation-failures -->
<p><strong>The timing that catches first shipments out.</strong> FDA prior notice must be filed and confirmed at least 8 hours before food arriving by water reaches the US port, and no more than 30 days before arrival when filed through ABI/ACE, under 21 CFR 1.279. The FSVP importer's name, email and unique facility identifier (a DUNS number is accepted) must be on each food line of the entry filed with US Customs and Border Protection (CBP). Scenario: a first container to Savannah arrives on time, but the consignee's broker has not filed prior notice and the importer has no DUNS number on the entry. The shrimp sits in a reefer at the port accruing charges while paperwork that could have been done three weeks earlier is completed. Settle who files what before the vessel sails.</p>
<!-- /insight -->
<p>Beyond FSMA specifically, FDA maintains import alerts that can place particular products or exporters with a violation history under automatic detention pending testing. We treat pre-shipment antibiotic residue and microbiological testing as standard practice for US-bound shipments rather than an optional add-on — it's cheaper than a detained container every time.</p>

<!-- price-behaviour:start -->
<!-- insight:price-mechanism -->
<h2>What determines the price of frozen shrimp</h2>
<p>We never quote a fixed per-kg price in the abstract, because too many variables change it. The main ones:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Count size and form.</strong> Larger counts (16/20, 21/25) and higher-yield forms (PD, PDTO) cost more per pound than smaller counts or HOSO/HLSO.</li>
  <li><strong>Glaze percentage.</strong> A 20%-glaze quote and a 0%-glaze quote on the same nominal weight are not the same amount of shrimp — always confirm the basis.</li>
  <li><strong>Farm-gate conditions.</strong> Feed costs and disease pressure in the growing season (typically peaking with harvest cycles from Andhra Pradesh and neighbouring states) move raw-material cost meaningfully year to year.</li>
  <li><strong>Freight and bunker cost.</strong> Reefer freight to US ports is a substantial share of landed cost, and bunker fuel surcharges move independently of the shrimp market itself.</li>
  <li><strong>Currency.</strong> Quotes are USD-denominated, but our cost base is INR, so USD/INR movement affects where we can price competitively.</li>
</ul>
<p>The US position in India's shrimp trade cuts both ways. The US took 311,948 MT in FY2024-25, 42% of India's frozen shrimp exports and more than double China's 136,164 MT. Indian packers depend on US demand, so a change in US demand or import terms moves Indian offers quickly. A US buyer has the most leverage on price when US demand is soft and the least when it is strong.</p>
<p>Quotes are issued FOB or CIF on stated net weight, with glaze percentage disclosed separately. To quote accurately we need: product form, count size, glaze target, packaging (carton configuration or IQF bulk), destination port, and order volume. Antibiotic-residue and microbiological testing requirements (if you want us to pre-test before shipment) also factor into lead time and cost.</p>
